---
title: Self-hosting
description: Run the full NeoWorks stack on your own machine with process-compose and Docker.
---

NeoWorks is MIT licensed and runs anywhere Docker does. Local development uses
**process-compose** to supervise the backing services and the app processes together.

## Prerequisites

- Docker
- [process-compose](https://github.com/F1bonacc1/process-compose)
- Bun and Go (for the app processes)
- Caddy (for local HTTPS on `*.neoworks.localhost`)

## Backing services

The infrastructure runs as individual `docker run` processes supervised by
process-compose — there is no monolithic compose file. Each service streams its logs
into the process-compose UI:

- **SurrealDB** — the multi-model database (`:8000`).
- **Redis** — session tokens, write-queue buffer, rate limiting (`:6379`).
- **NATS** — JetStream for real-time fan-out (`:4222`).
- **MinIO** — S3-compatible object storage (`:9000`, console `:9001`).

A one-shot `minio-setup` job creates the buckets, then an `infra` aggregator gates the
app processes until every service reports healthy.

## Start everything

```bash
process-compose up
```

This brings up the services, runs database migrations, then starts the `oauth`, `api`,
`web` and app processes behind Caddy.

## Data persistence

SurrealDB persists to a bind mount under `infra/data/surrealdb`, so your data survives
restarts. Redis, NATS, and MinIO use named Docker volumes.

## Shutting down

```bash
process-compose down
```

Each service is stopped with `docker stop`; because containers run with `--rm`, they are
removed cleanly on shutdown.
